+/-!
+
+# Racah's closed formula for the 6j symbol
+
+The operator-defined 6j symbol `sixJ` of `SixJ` equals Racah's closed formula `racah6j`.
+
+## i. Overview
+
+All spins are doubled naturals. For `0 < a`, `0 < c` and admissible `(K, k₁)`, `(K, k₃)`
+(`ValidA`, `ValidB`), `sixJ_eq_racah6j` states
+
+`{j₁ j₂ j₁₂; j₃ J j₂₃} = racah6j a b (a + b - 2 k₁) c (a + b + c - 2 K) (b + c - 2 k₃)`
+
+with `j₁ = a/2`, `j₂ = b/2`, `j₃ = c/2`, `j₁₂ = (a + b)/2 - k₁`, `j₂₃ = (b + c)/2 - k₃` and
+`J = (a + b + c)/2 - K`.
+
+1. In the region `k₁ + k₃ ≤ min b K`, where the third column of `{a b e; c g f}` has maximal sum,
+   `sixJ_eq_racah_R1` (`SixJRacahRegion`) gives Racah's formula for `sixJ` in the explicit shape
+   of `racah6j_eq_explicit`, and so, through `sixJ_eq_sixJSym`, for `sixJSym`
+   (`sixJSym_eq_racah6j_R1`).
+2. Both `racah6j` (`racah6j_swap₁₂`, `racah6j_swap₂₃`) and `sixJSym` (`sixJSym_swap₁₂`,
+   `sixJSym_swap₂₃`) are invariant under permutations of the columns. Moving the column of
+   maximal sum to the third place, and a column with two positive entries to the first, covers
+   every admissible tuple with `j₁ > 0` and `j₄ > 0` (`sixJSym_eq_racah6j`).
+3. `sixJ_eq_racah6j` is the same statement in the natural parameters of `SixJ`. It needs `0 < a`
+   and `0 < c`, like `sixJ_eq_sixJSym` and `sixJ_eq_const_mul_Xs`; the cases `a = 0` and `c = 0`
+   are not covered.
+
+## ii. Key results
+
+- `sixJ_eq_racah6j`: Racah's formula for `sixJ`.
+- `sixJSym_eq_racah6j`: Racah's formula for `sixJSym` on admissible tuples with `j₁, j₄ > 0`.
+- `TriadAdm`: admissibility of a triad in doubled units.
+
+## iii. Table of contents
+
+- A. The explicit form of `racah6j`
+- B. Racah's formula for `sixJSym`
+- C. Racah's formula for `sixJ`
+
+## iv. References
+
+- G. Racah, *Theory of complex spectra. II*, Phys. Rev. 62, 438 (1942).
+- NIST Digital Library of Mathematical Functions, §34.4, https://dlmf.nist.gov/34.4.
+
+-/
